# Docker Compose environment for the weftd stack.
#
# Compose reads this automatically (`docker compose up -d`). It holds the Postgres
# password, whether Caddy runs, and which ports get published. Everything else is
# application config in weft.toml / livekit.yaml / Caddyfile.
#
# The password is NOT copied into weft.toml: weftd expands `${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}`
# from the environment when it loads that file, so this is the only place it is
# written. An unset value fails the start instead of becoming an empty password.

# ── Secrets ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `openssl rand -hex 32`. Read by the postgres service and passed to weftd.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-postgres-password

# ── Which services start ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A comma-separated list of Compose profiles.
#   caddy  (default) → run the bundled reverse proxy: automatic Let's Encrypt on
#                      80/443, and — the part that matters beyond proxying — the
#                      certificate weftd needs for QUIC, which cannot be proxied.
#   (empty)          → no Caddy, for when something else already terminates TLS.
#                      Publish weftd's HTTP port yourself (below) and give weftd a
#                      certificate of its own: README.md → Standalone TLS.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=caddy

# ── weftd's HTTP port ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The web client, /ws, /.well-known/weft, /media — all plain HTTP.
#
#   • With Caddy (default): keep 127.0.0.1. Caddy reaches it from inside this
#     project by service name, so the published port only exists for debugging and
#     is not reachable from the network at all.
#   • Without Caddy: set 0.0.0.0 (or a specific address) to expose it, and put your
#     own TLS in front — or use it plain on a trusted LAN.
WEFT_HTTP_BIND=127.0.0.1
WEFT_HTTP_PORT=8081
